Downloading datasets with SRA toolkit

-----------------------------------
##########################################################
Step 1: Install the SRA Toolkit
##########################################################

The SRA Toolkit is a set of command-line tools developed by NCBI to download and convert sequencing data.

For macOS/Linux (most common for bioinformatics):

bash
# Download the latest version (replace with your OS)
wget https://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sdk/current/sratoolkit.current-ubuntu64.tar.gz
tar -vxzf sratoolkit.current-ubuntu64.tar.gz
# Add the bin directory to your PATH (or use the full path)
export PATH=$PATH:$PWD/sratoolkit.3.1.1-ubuntu64/bin
For Windows: You can install the SRA Toolkit via the Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) and follow the Linux instructions above, or download the Windows binary from the NCBI SRA Toolkit page.

##########################################################
Step 2: Find the Run Accession Numbers (SRR)
##########################################################

A BioProject (PRJNA) contains multiple "Runs" (SRR numbers), each corresponding to one sequenced sample. You need to get the list of SRR numbers for PRJNA882369.

Option A: Use the SRA Run Selector (Web Interface)

Go to the SRA Run Selector: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/Traces/study/

Enter PRJNA882369 in the search box.

You will see a table of all runs. Click the "Download" button and select "Accession List". This will download a file named SRR_Acc_List.txt containing all the SRR numbers.

Option B: Use the Command Line (E-utilities)

bash
# This queries NCBI's E-utilities API to get the run list
esearch -db sra -query PRJNA882369 | efetch -format runinfo | cut -d ',' -f 1 | grep SRR > SRR_Acc_List.txt
(Note: You may need to install the edirect package first.)

##########################################################
Step 3: Download the FASTQ Files
##########################################################

Once you have the SRR_Acc_List.txt file, use the SRA Toolkit to download and convert the data.

Recommended method (using prefetch + fasterq-dump):
# 1. Create a directory for the downloaded data
mkdir -p raw_data/fastq

# 2. Loop through each SRR in the list
while read -r run_id; do
    echo "Processing $run_id"
    # Download the .sra file
    prefetch "$run_id"
    # Convert .sra to FASTQ (split paired-end reads, compress output)
    fasterq-dump "$run_id" --split-files -e 8 -p -O raw_data/fastq/
    # Optional: compress the FASTQ files to save space
    gzip raw_data/fastq/"$run_id"*.fastq
done < SRR_Acc_List.txt

xplanation of flags:

--split-files: Separates paired-end reads into _1.fastq and _2.fastq files.

-e 8: Uses 8 threads for faster processing.

-p: Shows progress.

-O: Output directory.

Alternative (single command, but slower for large datasets):

fastq-dump --split-files --gzip SRR1234567



##########################################################
Step 4: Download the Metadata
##########################################################
You will also need the metadata (sample information, group labels, etc.) to run the R analysis.

Option A: From the SRA Run Selector

In the SRA Run Selector table, click "Metadata" to download a CSV file containing sample attributes.

Option B: From the publication

E.g. the paper (Chen et al., 2022) should have a supplementary table with the metadata. Check the journal's website or the "Supplementary Materials" link.

Option C: Using the command line
----------------------------
esearch -db sra -query PRJNA882369 | efetch -format runinfo > metadata.csv
-----------------------
This downloads a CSV with all run information, including sample attributes.

mportant Notes for Your Meta-Analysis
Study-specific primers: The authors mention removing study-specific primers. When you process the data with QIIME2, you will need to know the primer sequences used in this study (V3-V4 region). These are usually listed in the paper's Methods section.

Metadata standardization: When you create your unified metadata file (as discussed earlier), make sure to add a Set column (e.g., "Chen_2022") to distinguish this study from others.

File sizes: 16S rRNA datasets are usually manageable (a few GB per study), but downloading 30+ samples may take some time depending on your internet connection


